    "text": "© 2026 SaturdayDownSouth.com Home > News > Sports > How to Watch Vanderbilt Commodores vs Nebraska: NCAA Tournament Tip-Off on TNT Ad Disclosure This article/post contains references to products or services from one or more of our advertisers or partners. We may receive compensation when you click on links to those products or services. The NCAA Men’s Basketball postseason continues as the No. 5 Vanderbilt Commodores take on the No. 4 Nebraska Cornhuskers. Both programs enter this Round of 32 matchup making history. Vanderbilt recently secured its first tournament victory since 2012. Nebraska is celebrating its first ever NCAA Tournament win in program history. Head coach Mark Byington leads a 27-8 Vanderbilt squad into a tough environment. Nebraska coach Fred Hoiberg has his 27-6 team playing with heavy regional support. Watch Vanderbilt vs Nebraska on Sling. Although this is a neutral site game, the location makes it a true road test for Vanderbilt. Keep reading to find everything you need to follow this closely matched contest. We have all the details covered, including the official start time, the exact location, the television channel, and all the available streaming options to catch the broadcast. When is Vanderbilt vs Nebraska? What time does it Start? The postseason matchup between the Vanderbilt Commodores and the Nebraska Cornhuskers is scheduled for March 21, 2026. Tip-off is set for 8:45 PM ET (7:45 PM local CT). The game takes place at the Paycom Center in Oklahoma City. Where is Vanderbilt vs Nebraska? This NCAA Tournament game takes place at the Paycom Center. The arena is located in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma. Because this is a neutral site matchup, neither team is playing on its actual home court. However, for official bracketing purposes, the Nebraska Cornhuskers are the designated home team. The Vanderbilt Commodores serve as the away team. Coach Byington and the Vanderbilt players have noted the venue feels like a true road game due to the heavy local support for Nebraska. How can I Watch Vanderbilt vs Nebraska? Fans looking to tune into the game between the Vanderbilt Commodores and the Nebraska Cornhuskers can catch the national television broadcast on TNT. The network is providing full television coverage of the matchup as the two teams tip off in Oklahoma City. How can I Stream Vanderbilt vs Nebraska? Viewers have multiple digital options to stream the TNT broadcast. Fans with a qualifying cable or satellite subscription can stream the game directly through the TNT app or the network website. You just need to log in with your television provider credentials. If you use live television streaming platforms instead of traditional cable, TNT is carried by several major digital services, including Sling TV. Be sure to check your specific service package to confirm TNT is included in your channel lineup. Matchup Preview and Kalshi Predictions This game presents a fascinating stylistic clash. Nebraska relies on a stifling guard defense to disrupt opponents, while Vanderbilt leans on a resilient backcourt that recently overcame an early double digit deficit. The location clearly favors Nebraska, but Vanderbilt thrives with a proven road warrior mentality. When looking at the prediction markets on Kalshi, the forecast leans toward the SEC squad. Traders on Kalshi are currently backing Vanderbilt to advance, pricing their shares higher than Nebraska. The market activity on Kalshi indicates that users see a Vanderbilt victory as the more probable outcome. Who will Win Vanderbilt vs Nebraska? Data from Kalshi on March 20th. Based on the current implied probabilities, the Vanderbilt Commodores are the mathematical favorites to win this matchup. The data shows Vanderbilt with a 54% chance of securing the victory. The Nebraska Cornhuskers hold a 46% implied probability of advancing to the next round. While the margins are relatively close, the prediction markets suggest Vanderbilt holds the higher likelihood of moving forward in the tournament. Creative Manager and Writer at Sportradar. Craig has previously worked as a camera operator and video director in radio and television, as well as a content coordinator in the non-profit sector. You might also like...",
